Creating a grid layout system from scratch using HTML and CSS involves HTML structure, CSS styling, CSS flexbox for content centering and media queries for responsiveness. 

This is a generic version of how it can be done:

HTML:
```html
<div class="grid-container">
  <div class="grid-item">Content 1</div>
  <div class="grid-item">Content 2</div>
  <div class="grid-item">Content 3</div>
  <!-- replicate as many items as needed -->
</div>
```
CSS:
```css
.grid-container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));
  grid-gap: 10px;
}

.grid-item {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  background-color: #333;
  color: #fff;
  padding: 20px;
  text-align: center;
}
```
This makes a grid layout of items where each item will have minimum width of 200px and maximum available. They will properly rearrange themselves based on the available viewport size due to `auto-fill` and `minmax()` CSS functionalities.

You can modify `minmax(200px, 1fr)` to accommodate your specific design, where `200px` is the minimum width of each grid cell, and `1fr` (fractional unit) allows the cell to take up the remaining space in the viewport.

To center text, we use `display: flex;`, `justify-content: center;`, and `align-items: center;` on the grid items. These flexbox properties will center any content inside grid items, no matter how much content there is.

For responsiveness, use CSS media queries. Here's an example:

```css
@media only screen and (max-width: 600px) {
  .grid-container {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(100px, 1fr));
  }
}
```
In this example, when the screen size reaches 600px or less, the grid items' minimum width will become 100px instead of 200px, allowing more items per row and better usage of small screen space. Modify as necessary for your specific design.

Note: This code assumes the use of a modern CSS grid layout and may not be compatible with IE11 or older browsers. If you need to support older browsers, consider using a framework that includes a polyfill, or use another layout system like flexbox or floats.

