Let $L$ be a Lie algebra over a field $\mathbb{F}$. An $L$-module is defined as a finite-dimensional $\mathbb{F}$-vector space $V$ equipped with a bilinear action $L \times V \to V: (x, v) \mapsto x \circ v$ that satisfies the Jacobi identity:
\begin{equation*}
[x, y] \circ v = x \circ (y \circ v) - y \circ (x \circ v)
\end{equation*}
Texts on Lie algebras often state that this defines a representation $\rho: L \to \text{Lie}(\text{End}(V))$. However, why does the definition of an $L$-module imply that each element of $L$ can be associated with an endomorphism of $V$, and not necessarily an automorphism? Could the action of $L$ be non-injective, i.e., $x \circ v_1 = x \circ v_2$ for distinct $v_1, v_2 \in V$? Furthermore, what is the value of $\rho(0)$, and how does it relate to the action of $0$ on $V$?

