```javascript
function removeDuplicateCharacters(string) {
    const result = [];
    const length = string.length;

    for(let i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        const char = string[i];
        if (string.indexOf(char) === i) {
            result.push(char);
        }
    }

    return result.join('');
}

console.log(removeDuplicateCharacters("google"));
```

Explanation:

In this function, we iterate through the input string from the first character to the last. For each character, `string.indexOf(char)` gives us the index of its first occurrence in the string, and if this index is same as the current index `i`, it means this is the first occurrence of the character, so we push it to the `result` array. If the indexes are not the same, it means this character has occurred before, so we just ignore it.

Finally, we join the `result` array into a string with `result.join('')` and return it.

When we run the line `console.log(removeDuplicateCharacters("google"));` it prints "gole", because those are the characters from "google" in their first occurrence order, with duplicates removed.

