FND_STANDARD_DATE is a date format used in Oracle E-Business Suite (EBS) applications. It is a standardized date format that is used in all EBS modules for storing, processing and displaying date values. This format is composed of a 14-character string with the following syntax:

`YYYYMMDDHH24MISS`

Where:

- `YYYY` represents the year in four digits format.
- `MM` represents the month in two digits format (01-12).
- `DD` represents the day in two digits format (01-31).
- `HH24` represents the hour in 24-hour format (00-23).
- `MI` represents the minute in two digits format (00-59).
- `SS` represents the second in two digits format (00-59).

The FND_STANDARD_DATE format has some usage constraints. For example:

- The value of the year cannot be less than 1000 or greater than 9999.
- The value of the month must be between 01 and 12.
- The value of the day must be between 01 and 31, depending on the month and year.
- The value of the hour must be between 00 and 23.
- The value of the minute must be between 00 and 59.
- The value of the second must be between 00 and 59.

The usage of the FND_STANDARD_DATE format in different versions of Oracle EBS is mostly consistent. However, some versions may have additional constraints or conventions depending on the specific module or functionality. For example, in some versions, the FND_STANDARD_DATE format is used to represent only date values without the time component.

Below is a sample code illustrating how the FND_STANDARD_DATE format is used in a typical transaction process in Oracle EBS:

```sql
DECLARE
  l_date_str VARCHAR2(14);
  l_date DATE;
BEGIN
  -- Get the current system date and time
  l_date := SYSDATE;

  -- Convert the date to FND_STANDARD_DATE format
  l_date_str := TO_CHAR(l_date, 'YYYYMMDDHH24MISS');

  -- Insert the date into a table
  INSERT INTO my_table (my_date_col) VALUES (l_date_str);
END;
```

